WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.928 --> 00:00:08.588
little virtual soldiers to camp&nbsp;
and teaches them how to fight.

00:00:08.588 --> 00:00:15.428
This is a follow up work to an absolutely&nbsp;
amazing paper called ASE. This is a previous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.428 --> 00:00:24.008
work with little AI soldiers that trained&nbsp;
for 10 years. 10 years? How? 10 years of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.008 --> 00:00:29.228
their in-game world that is, which is&nbsp;
fortunately when using a fast machine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.228 --> 00:00:35.828
is only 10 days in our time. And they learned&nbsp;
a great deal. They went from a bunch of sorry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.828 --> 00:00:42.908
little recruits to battle-ready soldiers.&nbsp;
Except this chap. This chap goes like “Sir,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.908 --> 00:00:49.208
I’ve been training for two years, I’ve had&nbsp;
enough! And now, I shall leave… in style.”

00:00:50.228 --> 00:00:57.008
In this new work, these little soldiers trained&nbsp;
for 5 billion training steps on a single graphics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.008 --> 00:01:04.088
card. It’s not a cheap one, but still, a single&nbsp;
graphics card. So, what do we get for these 5&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.088 --> 00:01:10.208
billion training steps? Can this new one do&nbsp;
anything better than the previous one? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.208 --> 00:01:17.348
first, it can learn from unlabeled motion&nbsp;
capture data. What is that? Well, a bunch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.348 --> 00:01:23.048
of dots moving that represents movements&nbsp;
of real humans that have been recorded.

00:01:23.888 --> 00:01:30.668
Step number one. Basic training. Here, the&nbsp;
moving dots go in, and a neural network is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.668 --> 00:01:38.828
asked to create motions that are similar to it.
So, are we done? Well, not quite. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.828 --> 00:01:46.148
we wish to use this in video games, and this is&nbsp;
not controllable yet. Imagine a video game where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.148 --> 00:01:53.468
you press a button on the controller, and nothing&nbsp;
happens. So now comes step number two. Precision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.468 --> 00:02:00.248
training! Here, it now needs to perform the&nbsp;
movements, but also listen to our controls,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.248 --> 00:02:06.668
especially when we use the stick to steer it&nbsp;
in different directions. And step number three,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.668 --> 00:02:14.408
here we not only give it directions, but intuitive&nbsp;
commands as well, such as striking or running.

00:02:14.408 --> 00:02:19.808
So, all that is great, but what can it&nbsp;
really do now? Well, let’s see together.

00:02:19.808 --> 00:02:26.828
For instance, it can interpolate between two&nbsp;
movement types. This means that it works like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.828 --> 00:02:33.908
gymnast who has perfected a difficult routine. It&nbsp;
can't jump straight from one move to another. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.908 --> 00:02:40.808
needs to transition smoothly to maintain balance&nbsp;
and perform the routine successfully. Thus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.808 --> 00:02:48.308
it first starts running, and then, look, it&nbsp;
gradually dreams up a smooth transition from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.308 --> 00:02:55.328
sprinting to crouching. That is a really&nbsp;
nice and believable transition. Love it.

00:02:55.328 --> 00:03:00.008
And now comes the best part. Hold&nbsp;
on to your papers Fellow Scholars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.008 --> 00:03:06.248
because now you’ll see how it can do these&nbsp;
types of transitions on-demand. That means,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.248 --> 00:03:11.828
as a reaction to our button&nbsp;
mashing on the controller. So good!

00:03:11.828 --> 00:03:16.868
However, not even this technique is&nbsp;
perfect. It still has some issues to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.868 --> 00:03:20.108
be worked out. For instance, check this out. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.108 --> 00:03:26.828
little AI, move towards the target.&nbsp;
Attack, great and now, celebrate!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.548 --> 00:03:35.228
Whoa! Careful! Moments like this show that this&nbsp;
work is still experimental. I don’t think you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.228 --> 00:03:40.688
will see this in a new game tomorrow, but this&nbsp;
is what research is about. Taking something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.688 --> 00:03:46.628
highly unstructured and using learning-based&nbsp;
techniques to create something useful from it.

00:03:46.628 --> 00:03:54.848
Now, wait a second, in the intro, I also said&nbsp;
that this is a follow up to the ASE paper. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.848 --> 00:04:03.788
how does it compare to that one? Oh my. Are you&nbsp;
seeing what I am seeing? Because I am seeing two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.788 --> 00:04:10.148
excellent news here. Now, I hear you asking,&nbsp;
okay Doctor, what are the excellent news here?&nbsp;

00:04:10.148 --> 00:04:16.748
Well, one, let’s start with the most obvious&nbsp;
one - the controllability of the simulation has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.748 --> 00:04:23.408
improved leaps and bounds. That is incredible.&nbsp;
And second, what is even more incredible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.408 --> 00:04:31.148
this controllability did not come at the expense&nbsp;
of the diversity of the movesets. That is perhaps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.148 --> 00:04:37.088
the best part of the paper - you see, this is in&nbsp;
a class of neural network-based based techniques&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.088 --> 00:04:43.748
that often suffers from a phenomenon called&nbsp;
mode collapse. What is mode collapse? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.748 --> 00:04:50.888
imagine asking a gymnast to perform incredible&nbsp;
acrobatics, and this gymnast can do a variety&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.888 --> 00:04:58.448
of things, but unfortunately, just keeps doing&nbsp;
the same thing over and over again. Cartwheels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.448 --> 00:05:04.148
all day every day. Imagine looking at&nbsp;
an olympic gold-medal athlete who just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.148 --> 00:05:09.548
keeps doing cartwheels. And, it is not&nbsp;
the case here. This is an athlete that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.548 --> 00:05:15.608
can do everything. I absolutely love&nbsp;
this. Bravo! What a time to be alive!

00:05:15.608 --> 00:05:22.328
And finally, some fantastic news for the end -&nbsp;
the source code of this project is available.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.328 --> 00:05:27.668
I would like to send a big thank you to&nbsp;
the scientists on this project making it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.668 --> 00:05:33.428
available for all of us, free of charge.&nbsp;
And for now, let the experiments begin!

00:06:32.168 --> 00:06:36.428
